  "worked_solution": "The center of the expansion is $x=-1,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-4,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $3,$ i.e., $R\\ge 3.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[-4,2).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[-4,2).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041629/assets/a246a6460d15037b469072b4275e51ff4dd9c56242f6ffeae1bebc2adf66f39f.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $-5\\notin [-4,2),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is divergent at $x=-5.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $2\\notin [-4,2),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $-3\\in [-4,2),$ the series is convergent at $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
